## What an HOC is

A higher-order component is a function that takes a component and returns a new one wrapping it with extra behaviour:

```jsx
function withAuth(Component) {
  return function Guarded(props) {
    const user = useCurrentUser();
    if (!user) return <LoginPrompt />;
    return <Component {...props} user={user} />;
  };
}

const ProtectedPage = withAuth(Page);
```

The name comes from higher-order functions — functions that take or return functions.

## What it was used for

Before hooks, HOCs were the main way to share cross-cutting logic: auth gating, injecting data from a store (`connect` in React-Redux), subscriptions, logging. The pattern is composition — you wrap rather than edit.

## The downsides

Stacked HOCs produce deep wrapper nesting in the React DevTools. It is not obvious where an injected prop came from, prop names can collide, and typing them well is awkward.

## Hooks replaced most of them

A custom hook shares stateful logic without wrapping anything — `const user = useCurrentUser()` instead of `withAuth`. No extra tree nodes, clear data origin. HOCs still appear in older code and a few library APIs, but for new logic reuse, write a hook.

