## The pattern

A long-standing way to organise components by splitting two concerns.

**Presentational ("dumb")** — how things look. Takes props, renders markup, holds little or no state, has no idea where its data comes from:

```jsx
function UserList({ users, onSelect }) {
  return users.map((u) => (
    <li key={u.id} onClick={() => onSelect(u.id)}>{u.name}</li>
  ));
}
```

**Container ("smart")** — how things work. Fetches data, holds state, passes both down:

```jsx
function UserListContainer() {
  const { data } = useUsers();
  return <UserList users={data ?? []} onSelect={openProfile} />;
}
```

## Why split

Presentational components stay reusable and trivial to test — pure functions of props. They are what you put in a design system or Storybook. Logic concentrates in containers, so it is easier to find and change.

## How hooks changed it

The strict split mattered more in the class era. A custom hook now carries the data logic — `useUsers()` — so a single component can stay readable while doing both. Many teams no longer keep a literal container component, but the underlying idea — separate data logic from presentation — is still good design.

