## The problem it addresses

When state is shared across many parts of an app and changes in complex ways, scattering it across components and prop chains gets hard to follow. Redux centralises that state and makes every change go through one disciplined path.

## The three principles

- **Single source of truth** — all shared state in one store.
- **State is read-only** — you change it only by dispatching an action, a plain object describing what happened.
- **Changes are pure functions** — reducers take `(state, action)` and return the next state, with no side effects.

```js
function cartReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'cart/add':
      return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}
```

## What you get

Predictable, traceable updates; powerful debugging — time-travel, action logs, devtools; and logic that lives outside components, so it is easy to test.

## When it is worth it

Redux shines for large apps with genuinely complex shared state. For most apps it is more ceremony than needed — Context for simple sharing, Zustand for a lighter store, TanStack Query for server data. Reach for Redux when the app's state complexity actually justifies it.

